            <ArticleTitle>Connection between smoking and back pain--findings from an Icelandic general population study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>33-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The association between pain in general, back pain and smoking was studied in a sample of subjects from the general public (n = 862). The results show that smoking men had pain problems more frequently than women (p &lt; 0.0001). In comparison with non-smokers, smokers were found to have back pains more frequently (p &lt; 0.01). Male smokers were also found to have a higher frequency of intervertebral disc problems than female smokers (p &lt; 0.009). The frequency of disc problems encountered by the smoking subjects indicates a possible connection between smoking and back pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
